Z||000013||V8Fiero2000GP||11-11-2000||01:33 AM||||It would be a good idea for the Pontiac dealer in 2002 to have a mid engined sports car again.  Any type is better than no type.<BR>But it would be better if it was labeled as a sports car, not a commuter, this time.<BR>I read it will weigh 1900lbs. and have 147HP<BR>giving it a 5.9 sec 0-60 push.  Better than the Fiero GT.<P>As a 5.7L V8 Fiero owner, I still ask:<BR>Ferrari represents Italy, <BR>Porsche represents Germany,<BR>now Acura NSX represents Japan,<P>Where is our mid engine sports car?<BR>Pantera doesn't count.  It was made in Italy and was only called a Ford.  It was powered by Ford, but it shares nothing with the Mustang other than that.  Fiero shares lots of things with the Trans Am and GM cars, and it was the only production mid engine car in USA.  I can call it American, but we need a mid engined sports car to cary our flag.<P>Where is it? Viper and Corvettes don't count.<BR>They are not mid engined exotics.||||reg||14
Z||000014||V8Fiero2000GP||11-11-2000||01:46 AM||||I also agree with the comment from Dale above.  If Pontiac will do this again they should do it right.  Probably if the Opel car was high performance like:  Viper, Corvette, or Mustang Cobra, it would help to make it better all around.  If the "Opel" would be another fun and silly car like the Fiero, it will fail again, naturally.  The Pontiac GTO did not become and stay successful by being fun and silly.  It had to be made serious.||||reg||14
